"""The OpenID Connect provider this application hosts, on top of its authorization server.

One setting has no default the provider can invent: ``identity_provider``, the
application's class that finds a signed-in user and the claims they hold. Write
that class over wherever this application keeps its users, name it here, and the
bundle registers it — nothing registers it for you. Until it is named the
provider still builds, boots and serves the console; the userinfo endpoint is
what reports the setting, by name and with this file.

``login_path`` is where an end-user asked to sign in again is sent, and
``login_firewall`` the firewall that signs them in there — the one whose
``listeners`` carries ``openid_connect_server#prompt_listener``. It must keep a
session: the authorization request waits in it while the end-user signs in, and
a stateless firewall is refused by name the first time a re-login is asked of
it.

``authorize_path`` is where this deployment serves the authorization endpoint,
which is where the prompt step acts. Change it when a routes file moves that
endpoint under a prefix.

Everything else keeps its default: an ID token expires with the access token
issued beside it (``id_token_ttl``), and the only claim sets are the four OpenID
Connect Core defines. ``claim_sets`` adds the claims a scope of this
application's own reveals — ``{"company": ("company_id",)}`` — and may not
restate one of the protocol's four.

The README's *Configure* section has the rest.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

from xtr_dependency_injection import configure
from xtr_security_openid_connect_server.bundle import OpenIdConnectServerConfig

__all__ = ["openid_connect_server"]


@configure
def openid_connect_server() -> OpenIdConnectServerConfig:
    """Sign end-users in at the login page of the firewall that keeps their session."""
    return OpenIdConnectServerConfig(
        # identity_provider=MyIdentityProvider,
        login_path="/login",
        login_firewall="main",
        authorize_path="/authorize",
    )
